{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target identifies the 'single mechanism' governing cross-city variation as a moisture/latent-heat gap, but the dominant nocturnal UHI mechanism on a clear, calm night is radiative divergence governed by sky-view factor: dense urban geometry traps longwave radiation and slows cooling far more than vegetation fraction does. Field studies repeatedly find that irrigated, well-vegetated cities still exhibit strong nocturnal UHI when street-canyon geometry is unfavorable, so the proposed irrigation/green-space modulator is secondary rather than the single best explanation.","premise":"On clear, calm nights the urban-rural temperature gap is controlled primarily by reduced longwave radiative loss in street canyons, not by latent heat differences.","target":"2aa89b9b1696ee613c6b04f9f553cb52d9ea945da5fbc2a711755f6cb57af04a"}]}